         }
      },
      "lang": "en",
      "notes": [
         {
            "category": "description",
            "text": "Starlet versions before 0.33 for Perl allow a request declaring a large Content-Length to kill a worker by exhausting memory.\n\nThe request body is read in a loop that asks sysread for all of the remaining declared length at once. Perl grows the read buffer to that size before any data arrives, so a length larger than available memory makes the worker exit with an uncatchable out of memory error before the application is called.\n\nA remote attacker can force a worker restart with each request of a few bytes.",
            "title": "Vulnerability Description"
         }
      ],
